  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below average temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions are expected into the weekend. \n\nThe cold front has pushed south of the forecast area, and 50s\ndew points are steadily spreading southward. Any rain should\nstay south of the area today, although clouds may not clear the\nsouthern tier until later this afternoon. High pressure of \nCanadian origin will build from the upper Great Lakes today to \nNew England by Friday night. The cold advection on northerly\nwinds will support below normal highs in the upper 70s and \nlower 80s. Humidity will also be low, with dew points\npotentially dropping into the 40s today across the northern half\nof the CWA. Tonight will be the coolest for most areas with \ncalm winds and mostly clear skies. Any rural areas could drop \ninto the 50s, while the urban centers and bayshore remain in the\nmid 60s. Friday will feature more mid and high level cloud \ncover.\n\nAs the high becomes positioned to the northeast, an inverted\ntrough will develop along the Appalachian Mountains. This will\nhelp draw some moisture back northward from the stalled frontal\nzone across the southeastern states. Shortwave troughs in the\nnorthwest flow aloft could combine with the convergence in the\nsurface trough to produce a few showers at times Friday \nafternoon through Saturday night. This rain is most probable \nwest of I-81, but a few showers across the southern tier can't \nbe ruled out depending on how the stationary front to our south \nbuckles. While low temperatures will start creeping up in \nresponse to higher dew points, Saturday's high temperatures \nshould remain below normal.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Returning to a summer-like pattern next week.\n\nUL troffing will persist through much of next week over the NE \nUS. S/WVs will move through the broad NW H5 flow with some \nguidance developing a cut-off low over NE towards the middle of \nnext week. Regardless of UL evolution, a return to a summer-like\npattern is favored with a notable increase in PWs/sfc RH. Heat \nbuilds gradually as well, but nothing near headline levels at \nthis time. The increase in heat and moisture will increase \ninstability and t-storm chances. Rain chances increase gradually\neach day through Tue (when there is some consensus a cdfnt could\napproach), but admittedly varies which day is highest between \nguidance. ML guidance highlights Tue as the highest day for SVR \nwx, though low probs exist almost daily.",
